{
  "mn137:0.1": "Middle Discourses 137 ",
  "mn137:0.2": "The Analysis of the Six Sense Fields ",
  "mn137:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "mn137:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. ",
  "mn137:1.3": "There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, ",
  "mn137:1.4": "“Mendicants!” ",
  "mn137:1.5": "“Venerable sir,” they replied. ",
  "mn137:1.6": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "mn137:2.1": "“Mendicants, I shall teach you the analysis of the six sense fields. ",
  "mn137:2.2": "Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” ",
  "mn137:2.3": "“Yes, sir,” they replied. ",
  "mn137:2.4": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "mn137:3.1": "“‘The six interior sense fields should be understood. The six exterior sense fields should be understood. The six classes of consciousness should be understood. The six classes of contact should be understood. The eighteen mental preoccupations should be understood. The thirty-six positions of sentient beings should be understood. Therein, relying on this, give up that. The Noble One cultivates the establishment of mindfulness in three cases, by virtue of which they are a Teacher worthy to instruct a group. Of all meditation tutors, it is he that is called the supreme guide for those fit for training.’ ",
  "mn137:3.2": "This is the summary recital for the analysis of the six sense fields. ",
  "mn137:4.1": "‘The six interior sense fields should be understood.’ ",
  "mn137:4.2": "That’s what I sa